## Knowledge Inference
- “Latent Knowledge Estimation”
- Latent - means “not directly measurable”

## WHY IS IT USEFUL TO MEASURE STUDENT KNOWLEDGE?
- Enhancing student knowledge is the primary goal of a lot of education
- If you can measure it, you know whether you're making it better
- If you can measure it, you can inform instructors (and other stakeholders) about it
- If you can measure it, you can make automated pedagogical decisions

## DIFFERENT FROM MEASURING PERFORMANCE
- Infer student performance right now associated with success in demonstrating a skill
## - NOT THE SAME AS “STUDENT HAS THE SKILL?”
- guess - student appeared to demonstrate but not have skill
- slip - student appeared to not demonstrate but have it

## HOW TO GET AT LATENT KNOWLEDGE?
- Cant measure it directly
- Cant look into brain

- Look at PERFORMANCE
- Look at Performance over Time
- More info that performance at on specific moment
- Can recgnize patterns
- Is the performance consistent on a specific skill?
- See where person struggles based on continuous failure on a
specific skill over time


## BAYESIAN KNOWLEDGE TRACING
- Classic approach to measure tightly defined skill in online learning
## - Richard Atkinson

- Albert Corbett and John Anderson
- Still most used
## - Interpretable
## - Predictable
## - Decent Performance
## -
## - KEY GOAL:
- Measure how well student know a specific skill/knowledge component at a
specific time
- Based on past history of performance (by skill/KC)

## SKILLS SHOULD BE TIGHTLY DEFINED
- Do not measure overall skill (arithmetic)
- Measure SPECIFIC SKILL (addition of 2 numbers with no carry)

## TYPICAL USE OF BKT?
- Assess student knowledge of skill/KC X
- Sequence of items -> dichotomously scored (dichotomously = binary => 0 or 1)
- Assign one target skill to each scored item. A minigame may cover several skills. For example, Toss-a-Lapi can contain separate items for prefixes, infixes, suffixes, and circumfixes.
- Student can learn each item with help, feedback, scaffolding, etc

